{
 "question": "What does the bit allocation do where enhanced coupling begins at its lowest sub-band, so that the coupling channel begins at band 13 of the masking model and the channels coupled into it end there?",
 "method": "No stream of the corpus or of a library uses enhanced coupling, and `oadec eac3-ecpl-inject` keeps the start of the coupling it rewrites, bin 37 or later. An encoder that writes the tool was found: MainConcept TotalCode Studio 5.3 (its Dolby Digital Plus Professional encoder, component 9.8.13), driven from its command line with a preset exported from its interface: E-AC-3, 48 kHz, 2/0, 64 kbit/s, dialogue normalisation -24, both compression profiles off. At that rate it couples from sub-band 0 (`ecplbegf` 0) in every frame and extends the spectrum above. Four signals were made with FFmpeg's `lavfi` sources, encoded, and the elementary stream taken out of the MP4 with `ffmpeg -c copy -f eac3`. Each stream was put to `verify --json` with four binaries: main before the change, the change, and the change with one rule taken out by the mutation of `tools/mutants.py` that names it. The Dolby decode is `gst-launch-1.0 filesrc ! dlbac3parse ! dlbac3dec drc-suppress=true out-ch-config=0 ! audio/x-raw,format=F32LE ! filesink` of Dolby Reference Player 3.2; `tools/band_compare.py --channels 2 --edges 1200 7950 12450` compares `oadec decode --format wav` with it in the three regions the streams code differently: the bins each channel codes itself (to 1 219 Hz), the coupling channel (to 7 969 Hz) and spectral extension (to 12 469 Hz). `tools/old_new.py --decode-all` compared the two binaries over the corpus, the four streams among it. The streams are media and are not in the repository; their digests are below.",
 "binaries": {
  "old": "767cd592c1c520b372b0dfa43fa4362901e7d2523edf7dadd69fcba972f157d2",
  "new": "d09ad21412de39ebffe7a333edba4b397d5d9a8197a23eebe70fb17ddf8440c8",
  "the first rule alone": "c537f2058368d0f111b274aa8fdd7ed3ca838fe92de104d7bb97534ec04cde28",
  "the second rule alone": "5426c6c02b86b3133d7dabf7ab112fd4e2bf6e6d52f8e547cd0b6f1786e9e0fc"
 },
 "result": {
  "the_two_rules": {
   "the first": "the coupling channel has the low-frequency compensation while its bands are below 22: `calc_lowcomp` runs from zero and the excitation is `max(fastleak - lowcomp, slowleak)`",
   "the second": "the last band of a channel looks at no band after it: `calc_lowcomp` is left out there, as clause 6.2.2.4 leaves it out for the last band of the LFE channel",
   "where they show": "only with `ecplbegf` 0: the coupling channel begins at bin 13 (band 13) and the channels end there. With `ecplbegf` 1 it begins at bin 25, past band 22, as with every start of standard coupling, and both rules give what the clause prints"
  },
